For the <displacement gradient tensor> specified in the question,
$$
\nabla\mathbf u:\nabla\mathbf u^T
=\operatorname{tr}(\nabla\mathbf u\,\nabla\mathbf u^T)
=u_x^2+u_y^2+v_x^2+v_y^2,
$$
while the <divergence> is $\nabla\cdot\mathbf u=u_x+v_y$. Hence the <isotropic linear-elastic energy density> is
$$
\frac\mu2(u_x^2+u_y^2+v_x^2+v_y^2)
+\frac{\lambda+\mu}{2}(u_x+v_y)^2.
$$
Expanding the square and collecting terms yields
$$
\boxed{
\mathcal J=\iint_\Omega\left[
\left(\frac\lambda2+\mu\right)(u_x^2+v_y^2)
+\frac\mu2(u_y^2+v_x^2)
+(\lambda+\mu)u_xv_y
\right]dx\,dy}.
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let the displayed integrand be $F$. Since it has no explicit dependence on $u$ or $v$, the <Euler-Lagrange equations for two fields> are
$$
\partial_xF_{u_x}+\partial_yF_{u_y}=0,
\qquad
\partial_xF_{v_x}+\partial_yF_{v_y}=0.
$$
Its derivatives are
$$
F_{u_x}=(\lambda+2\mu)u_x+(\lambda+\mu)v_y,
\qquad F_{u_y}=\mu u_y,
$$
$$
F_{v_x}=\mu v_x,
\qquad F_{v_y}=(\lambda+2\mu)v_y+(\lambda+\mu)u_x.
$$
Thus
$$
(\lambda+2\mu)u_{xx}+\mu u_{yy}
+(\lambda+\mu)v_{xy}=0,
$$
$$
\mu v_{xx}+(\lambda+2\mu)v_{yy}
+(\lambda+\mu)u_{xy}=0.
$$
Using the <Laplacian>, <gradient>, and <divergence>, these combine into the static <Navier-Cauchy equation>
$$
\boxed{\mu\nabla^2\mathbf u
+(\lambda+\mu)\nabla(\nabla\cdot\mathbf u)=0}.
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

In the one-dimensional limit, $v=0$ and $u=u(x)$. The first component of the <Navier-Cauchy equation> reduces to
$$
(\lambda+2\mu)u''(x)=0.
$$
For a <nondegenerate one-dimensional elastic material>, $\lambda+2\mu\ne0$, so $u''=0$ and $u(x)=Ax+B$. The <boundary conditions> $u(0)=0$ and $u(L)=\Delta$ give $B=0$ and $A=\Delta/L$. Therefore the <uniform extension of a one-dimensional elastic body> is
$$
\boxed{u(x)=\frac{\Delta}{L}x}.
$$

