No. Take $L=\{<\}$ and let $T$ be the theory of linear orders. It has finite models of every positive size. Every finite linear order is a <rigid first-order structure>: an order automorphism fixes the least element, then the next element, and inductively every element.

However, $(\mathbb Z,<)$ is an infinite model of $T$ and is not rigid. For example,
$$
n\longmapsto n+1
$$
is a nonidentity order automorphism. Hence arbitrarily large rigid finite models do not force all infinite models to be rigid.

